# @spectrum-ts/convex

A [Convex](https://convex.dev) component for [Spectrum](https://photon.codes/spectrum) iMessage.

Convex can't run Spectrum's `for await (… of app.messages)` loop — that needs a long-lived process. And the usual webhook escape hatch doesn't fit either: `app.webhook()` dispatches its handler *after* the HTTP response, but a Convex isolate is frozen the moment the response is returned.

This component inverts that. A delivery is verified and written to a table **before** the 200 goes back, so an acknowledged message is already durable. Everything after that runs on Convex's scheduler.

It also ships the production pipeline from Photon's [architecture guide](https://photon.codes/docs/best-practices/architecture) — burst debouncing, in-flight cancellation, carry-forward, and idempotent paced sends — rather than leaving you to rebuild it.

## Install

```sh
npm i @spectrum-ts/convex
```

```ts
// convex/convex.config.ts
import { defineApp } from "convex/server";
import spectrum from "@spectrum-ts/convex/convex.config";

const app = defineApp();
app.use(spectrum);
export default app;
```

Set these in the Convex dashboard:

| Variable | Where | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `SPECTRUM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | app | Verifies the native webhook's HMAC |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_ID` | app | Spectrum Cloud project |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_SECRET` | app | Spectrum Cloud secret |

## Use

```ts
// convex/spectrum.ts
import { Spectrum } from "@spectrum-ts/convex";
import { components, internal } from "./_generated/api";

export const spectrum = new Spectrum(components.spectrum, {
  onBatch: internal.agent.respond,
  sender: internal.sender.deliver,
  debounceMs: 5000,
  pacingMs: 800,
});
```

```ts
// convex/http.ts — POST https://<deployment>.convex.site/spectrum/webhook
import { httpRouter } from "convex/server";
import { spectrum } from "./spectrum";

const http = httpRouter();
spectrum.registerRoutes(http);
export default http;
```

```ts
// convex/agent.ts — one invocation per settled burst
export const respond = internalAction({
  args: { spaceId: v.string(), chainId: v.string(), messages: v.array(v.any()), carried: v.array(v.any()) },
  handler: async (ctx, args) => {
    const reply = await generate(args.messages, args.carried);

    // The scheduler can't interrupt a running action, so re-check after
    // anything slow.
    if (await spectrum.isCancelled(ctx, args)) return null;

    await spectrum.send(ctx, { spaceId: args.spaceId, chainId: args.chainId, content: { type: "text", text: reply } });
    await spectrum.completeChain(ctx, args);
    return null;
  },
});
```

See [`example/`](./example) for a complete app, including the sender.

## How the pipeline behaves

```
webhook ──▶ verify + enqueue (durable) ──▶ 200
                     │
                     ├─ dedupe on message id (deliveries are at-least-once)
                     └─ (re)arm the debounce timer
                                │
              debounce expires ─▶ flush: drain the burst, start a chain
                                        │
                                        ▶ your onBatch handler
                                                │
                                                ▶ outbox ─▶ sender ─▶ iMessage
```

- **Bursts collapse.** "hey" → "wait" → "actually" arrives as one batch, not three replies.
- **Messages are never dropped.** Rows stay in the queue until a flush drains them, so a cancelled flush costs nothing. Anything drained-but-unanswered moves to `carried` and is prepended to the next batch as prior context.
- **Cancellation is per-chain.** A superseded turn can't talk over the one that replaced it. Liveness is decided by chain *identity*, not by comparing timestamps — two pipeline events can land in the same millisecond.
- **Sends are idempotent.** Each carries a `clientGuid` derived from `(chainId, seq)`, re-used verbatim on retry, so a send that landed before a lost acknowledgement can't post twice.

## Why the sender lives in your app

iMessage sends speak gRPC over HTTP/2. Convex's default V8 runtime can't do that, and a `"use node"` file may contain only actions — while the component is queries and mutations. So the transport is a small action **you** own, and the component calls it through a function handle:

```ts
// convex/sender.ts
"use node";
import { createCloudSender } from "@spectrum-ts/convex/sender";

const sender = createCloudSender(); // built once per container, not per send
```

That indirection is deliberate. The component depends on a `Sender` contract, not on gRPC — if Spectrum Cloud adds an HTTPS send endpoint, a fetch-based sender drops in and the whole pipeline moves into the V8 runtime with no schema or API change.

## Development

```sh
npm install
npm run codegen   # regenerate src/component/_generated
npm test
npm run build
```

> **Note:** `package.json` currently points `@spectrum-ts/core` and `@spectrum-ts/imessage` at local `file:` paths, and `tsconfig.json` maps their types to the built `.d.ts`. That's a temporary bridge while this component's required subpaths — `@spectrum-ts/core/webhook` and `@spectrum-ts/imessage/remote` — are unreleased. Restore the published semver ranges once they ship.

## License

MIT © [Photon](https://photon.codes)

---
title: "Convex"
description: "Run a Spectrum iMessage agent on Convex with a durable inbound pipeline"
---

{% set webhookRoute = "/spectrum/webhook" %}
{% set spectrumInstance = symbol("ts:spectrum-ts#SpectrumInstance") %}

[Convex](https://convex.dev) is a reactive backend whose functions are
request-scoped, so it cannot host the long-lived `for await (… of app.messages)`
loop that a {{ spectrumInstance | typeRef }} normally runs.

Webhooks are the usual answer for that — but `app.webhook()` dispatches its
handler **after** the HTTP response, and on Convex the isolate is frozen the
moment that response is returned. The handler would never finish.

The [`@spectrum-ts/convex`](https://github.com/photon-hq/spectrum-convex)
component inverts the order: a delivery is verified and written to a table
*before* the `200` is sent, so an acknowledged message is already durable.
Everything after that runs on Convex's scheduler.

It also ships the pipeline from [Architecture](/best-practices/architecture) and
[Inbound pipeline](/best-practices/inbound-pipeline) — debouncing, cancellation,
carry-forward, idempotent paced sends — as component tables and scheduled
functions, so you don't rebuild it per project.

## Install

```bash
npm i @spectrum-ts/convex
```

```typescript
// convex/convex.config.ts
import { defineApp } from "convex/server";
import spectrum from "@spectrum-ts/convex/convex.config";

const app = defineApp();
app.use(spectrum);
export default app;
```

Set three environment variables in the Convex dashboard:

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `SPECTRUM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | Verifies each delivery's HMAC |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_ID` | Spectrum Cloud project |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_SECRET` | Spectrum Cloud secret |

## Wire it up

```typescript
// convex/spectrum.ts
import { Spectrum } from "@spectrum-ts/convex";
import { components, internal } from "./_generated/api";

export const spectrum = new Spectrum(components.spectrum, {
  onBatch: internal.agent.respond,
  sender: internal.sender.deliver,
  debounceMs: 5000,
  pacingMs: 800,
});
```

```typescript
// convex/http.ts
import { httpRouter } from "convex/server";
import { spectrum } from "./spectrum";

const http = httpRouter();
spectrum.registerRoutes(http);
export default http;
```

Point your Spectrum Cloud webhook at
`https://<deployment>.convex.site{{ webhookRoute }}`.

## Handle a turn

`onBatch` is invoked once per settled burst, not once per message.

```typescript
// convex/agent.ts
export const respond = internalAction({
  args: {
    spaceId: v.string(),
    chainId: v.string(),
    messages: v.array(v.any()),
    carried: v.array(v.any()),
  },
  handler: async (ctx, args) => {
    const reply = await generate(args.messages, args.carried);

    // `ctx.scheduler.cancel` cannot stop an action that is already running, so
    // re-check after anything slow and bail out if a newer message arrived.
    if (await spectrum.isCancelled(ctx, args)) {
      return null;
    }

    await spectrum.send(ctx, {
      spaceId: args.spaceId,
      chainId: args.chainId,
      content: { type: "text", text: reply },
    });
    await spectrum.completeChain(ctx, args);
    return null;
  },
});
```

`carried` holds messages an earlier, cancelled turn had already drained. They
are older than `messages`, so present them as prior context (`[Earlier message]
…`) rather than as part of the current question.

<Note>
Native webhook deliveries are at-least-once. The component dedupes on the
provider message id before anything else runs, so a redelivery neither stores a
second row nor restarts the debounce window.
</Note>

## Sending

Sends go through a durable outbox. Each row carries a `clientGuid` derived from
`(chainId, seq)`, passed to the provider as its idempotency key and re-used
verbatim on retry — so a send that landed just before a lost acknowledgement
cannot post twice.

The transport itself lives in an action **your app** owns:

```typescript
// convex/sender.ts
"use node";

import { createCloudSender } from "@spectrum-ts/convex/sender";

const sender = createCloudSender();
```

<Warning>
iMessage sends speak gRPC over HTTP/2, which Convex's default V8 runtime cannot
do — and a `"use node"` file may contain only actions, while the component is
queries and mutations. So the sender cannot live inside the component. Build it
once per module (not per send) so warm containers skip the token mint and
channel handshake.
</Warning>

Inbound attachments work the same way: the webhook delivers metadata only, and
`fetchAttachment()` pulls the bytes over gRPC from a Node action. Store them
with `ctx.storage.store()` — Convex documents cap at 1 MiB, so bytes belong in
file storage.

## What you get

- **Bursts collapse into one turn.** Four messages in eight seconds produce one
  reply that saw all four, not four overlapping replies.
- **Nothing is dropped on cancellation.** Messages stay queued until a flush
  drains them; anything drained but unanswered is carried into the next batch.
- **A superseded turn cannot talk over its replacement.** Liveness is decided by
  chain identity rather than by comparing timestamps, so it stays correct even
  when two pipeline events land in the same millisecond.

